    "result_text": "I implemented `format_for_display` in `app/report.py` and added tests in `tests/test_report.py`. The full suite passes (4 tests).\n\n**How it works:** it formats each cell with `table.map(...)`, which returns a new table and leaves the input unchanged. The index and columns stay the same. (`DataFrame.applymap` no longer exists in the pinned pandas 3.0.6, so `map` is the right call here.)\n\n- `12500` \u2192 `\"12,500.00\"`\n- `-634.2` \u2192 `\"(634.20)\"`\n- `0` \u2192 `\"-\"`\n- missing \u2192 `\"\"`\n\n**One choice you might want to change:** amounts are rounded to cents before the zero check. Sums of floats can leave tiny leftovers like `5.5e-17`, and this way they show as `\"-\"` instead of `\"0.00\"` or `\"(0.00)\"`. The catch is that any amount under half a cent, such as `-0.004`, also shows as `\"-\"`.\n\n**Tests:** they run against the real ledger data and check four things:\n- the formatted values, including a negative (Salaries \u2192 `\"(8,200.00)\"`) and a missing cell (Travel in July \u2192 `\"\"`)\n- the index and columns match the input\n- the input table is unchanged\n- every cell is a string\n\nA second test covers zero, NaN, float leftovers, millions and sub-cent negatives."
  },
